---
import Icon from './Icon.astro';
import '../styles/resource-methods.css';
const record = [
  ['Hardware configuration', 'CPU, RAM, graphics, peripherals and power settings'],
  ['Measurement boundary', 'PC only, or name every device included at the socket'],
  ['Observation duration', 'Start/end time and elapsed hours; warm-up recorded separately'],
  ['Recorded energy', 'Start/end kWh readings and their difference'],
  ['Average wall power', '1,000 × interval kWh ÷ elapsed hours, in watts'],
  ['Idle or active state', 'Mining stopped, or exact workload/version/profile'],
  ['Measurement notes', 'Meter model/resolution, background work, temperatures; mark unknowns'],
];
---
<section class="measurement-guide" aria-labelledby="power-note-title">
  <header class="method-heading"><span class="eyebrow">MEASURE / THEN ESTIMATE</span><h2 id="power-note-title" tabindex="-1">What power value should I enter?</h2><p>Use average <strong>whole-PC power at the wall</strong> for the running-cost estimate above. CPU package telemetry and a processor's TDP are not a measurement of the complete computer's electricity use.</p></header>
  <figure class="power-boundary" aria-labelledby="power-diagram-caption">
    <div class="power-path">
      <div class="power-stage"><svg viewBox="0 0 64 64" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><rect x="15" y="6" width="34" height="52" rx="8"/><circle cx="26" cy="29" r="2"/><circle cx="38" cy="29" r="2"/><path d="M28 43h8M32 11v5"/></svg><strong>Wall socket</strong><span>AC electricity</span></div>
      <span class="power-wire" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <div class="power-stage power-meter"><Icon name="gauge" size="feature"/><strong>Plug-in meter</strong><span>Whole-PC W / kWh</span></div>
      <span class="power-wire" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <div class="pc-boundary"><span class="eyebrow">INSIDE THE PC</span><div class="psu-stage"><Icon name="lightning" size="diagram"/><strong>Power supply</strong><span>Conversion losses included at the wall</span></div><div class="component-boundary"><span><Icon name="cpu" size="diagram"/><strong>CPU</strong><small>Package telemetry: a narrower boundary</small></span><span><Icon name="circuitry" size="diagram"/><strong>Other components</strong><small>RAM, board, GPU, storage and fans</small></span></div></div>
    </div>
    <figcaption id="power-diagram-caption">The meter sits between the socket and the PC power supply: it includes the supply's losses and everything powered by that PC. A CPU sensor describes a component, not this whole-system boundary. A separately plugged-in monitor is outside the PC reading unless deliberately included and recorded.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="measurement-columns">
    <section aria-labelledby="measure-workflow"><h3 id="measure-workflow" tabindex="-1">A repeatable wall-power reading</h3><ol class="measurement-steps"><li><strong>Choose the boundary.</strong> Use an intact plug-in meter rated for your supply and load, following its manufacturer's instructions. Do not open equipment, modify mains wiring or exceed ratings. Record which devices it powers.</li><li><strong>Choose a representative interval.</strong> Let the intended workload settle, then note elapsed time and start/end energy readings. Include normal variations; record warm-up separately. A single instantaneous watt reading can miss changes.</li><li><strong>Average the interval.</strong> Divide the energy difference by elapsed hours, then multiply by 1,000 to get watts. Use a long enough interval for the meter's resolution; record precision and repeat if readings vary. No universal interval suits every meter or workload.</li><li><strong>Keep the context.</strong> Note workload, settings and other activity. For mining comparisons, use the <a href="/troubleshoot/xmrig-low-hashrate/#keep-a-small-measurement-record">comparable-run worksheet</a> and match the hashrate and energy windows.</li></ol></section>
    <section aria-labelledby="watts-energy"><h3 id="watts-energy" tabindex="-1">Power is a rate. Energy accumulates.</h3><p><strong>Watts (W)</strong> describe power at a moment or an average over an interval. <strong>Kilowatt-hours (kWh)</strong> measure energy used over time. TDP is a processor design specification, not a wall-meter result for your workload.</p><div class="arithmetic-note"><span class="eyebrow">HYPOTHETICAL ARITHMETIC · NOT HARDWARE DATA</span><p>Suppose a meter records <strong>0.09 kWh over 30 minutes</strong>. That is 0.5 hours:</p><p class="method-equation">0.09 ÷ 0.5 × 1,000 = <strong>180 W average</strong></p><p>Holding that average for 8 hours would use 1.44 kWh. At an invented £0.25/kWh, the estimated cost is £0.36. The future hours and tariff are assumptions; the numbers here are a teaching example, not measurements.</p></div></section>
  </div>
  <section class="power-states" aria-labelledby="idle-active-title"><h3 id="idle-active-title" tabindex="-1">Total running cost or extra mining cost?</h3><div class="state-comparison"><div><span class="eyebrow">TOTAL ACTIVE POWER</span><p>Use the whole active PC reading for its running cost. This includes its baseline consumption as well as the workload.</p></div><div><span class="eyebrow">ACTIVE MINUS MATCHED IDLE</span><p>The difference estimates extra load relative to that idle state. Keep devices, settings and observation conditions matched; if the difference is within meter uncertainty, it is inconclusive.</p></div></div><p>Idle means the defined comparison state with mining stopped, not zero watts. If the PC would otherwise be switched off, a logged-in idle baseline is not the alternative. Do not silently substitute incremental watts for total watts in the calculator above.</p></section>
  <section aria-labelledby="manual-tariff"><h3 id="manual-tariff" tabindex="-1">Use your own tariff's unit rate</h3><p>Take the electricity <strong>price per kWh</strong> from your current bill, contract or supplier account, including applicable taxes. Keep it in the selected currency: GBP pence/kWh needs the pence option; £0.25/kWh and 25p/kWh describe the same rate. Currency selection does not convert money.</p><p>For peak/off-peak rates, calculate each period with its own rate and runtime and add the costs. Standing charges are normally payable independently of this PC's use; the calculator does not include them. A new supply or contract solely for mining changes that cost boundary. <a href="https://www.ofgem.gov.uk/your-energy-supply/your-energy-bill/energy-price-cap-unit-rates-and-standing-charges" rel="external noreferrer">Ofgem explains regional and multi-rate tariff context</a> for Great Britain; it is not a universal UK price or your personal tariff.</p></section>
  <section class="record-sheet" aria-labelledby="power-record-title"><header><span class="eyebrow">REUSE / COPY OR PRINT</span><h3 id="power-record-title" tabindex="-1">A small measurement record</h3></header><p>Copy these labels into your own notes, or print this page. Leave missing measurements as <strong>unknown</strong>; no values are stored or uploaded here.</p><dl class="measurement-record">{record.map(([field, note])=><div><dt>{field}</dt><dd>{note}<span class="record-space">Record: __________________ / unknown</span></dd></div>)}</dl></section>
  <p class="method-sources">Method references checked 25 November 2026: <a href="https://energysavingtrust.org.uk/energy-jargon-buster" rel="external noreferrer">Energy Saving Trust: power and energy units</a>; <a href="https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/support/articles/000055611/processors.html" rel="external noreferrer">Intel: TDP is a thermal design target</a>. The averaging and record workflow is Ember's proposed measurement method, not a report of hardware testing.</p>
</section>
